WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.592 --> 00:00:02.550
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:03.450
I'm Dustin Kirkland.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.870
I'm here with David Aronchick,
product manager for Kubeflow

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.550
at Google.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.760
And I'm really interested
in your insight

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.430
into the Kubeflow project.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.750
DAVID ARONCHICK: Yeah.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.870
It has been an
incredibly wild ride.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.750
I first came to Kubernetes
two and 1/2 years ago.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:25.770
I was, I like to say, the first
non-founding product manager

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:26.680
of Kubernetes.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.030
And I managed the platform
for two and 1/2 years.

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:33.780
And I was lucky enough to see
all the growth and evolution

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.510
of the Kubernetes ecosystem.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.330
After that time, I went
and took a step back

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.420
and said, well, geez, what's
the next hard problem here?

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.360
And it really is
not Kubernetes--

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.580
not that there's not an enormous
amount to do in the core

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.050
Kubernetes platform--

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:50.550
but it's really,
how do we empower

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.920
the next level of
people on top of it

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.900
using all the elements in the
platform that we built out?

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.780
I went out and talked
to customers, and users,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.000
and data scientists.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.140
And they all said,
"We would love

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.760
to do what you're doing with
Kubernetes but with ML and AI.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.940
And so that really was,
how do we make it portable?

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.650
How do we make it composable?

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.009
How do we make it declarative?

00:01:15.009 --> 00:01:17.550
In the same way that you can do
with Kubernetes applications,

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.890
you can do with this
very complex stack.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.110
And so we brought
it together and made

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.450
our initial announcement
in December.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.741
And we're very proud to have
announced our 0.1 release.

00:01:28.741 --> 00:01:30.990
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Was that
in the keynote this morning?

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.350
DAVID ARONCHICK: At the
keynote this morning, exactly.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:33.120
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
what are the highlights

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.310
of the keynote this morning,
if you can recap for us here?

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:36.476
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:01:36.476 --> 00:01:41.340
So as I mentioned, we had our
first official 0.1 release.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.190
That's a cut release,
meaning we do all the testing

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:47.610
and have zero outstanding P0, P1
bugs, and all that good stuff,

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.310
helping things get to
really production ready.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:53.280
We announced all the partners
and contributors who've

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.020
been adding to the platform.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.000
We have over 15
major corporations

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.280
who are contributing
to the core platform,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.489
700 commits in the
past three months.

00:02:01.489 --> 00:02:03.405
We're really really
excited with the momentum.

00:02:03.405 --> 00:02:05.640
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Do you want to
spotlight a few of those here?

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:06.720
DAVID ARONCHICK: Sure.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.940
Red Hat has it
running on OpenShift.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.780
Weave Works has contributed
monitoring to it.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.560
DaoCloud in China has done
not just contributions

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.540
to the overall
TensorFlow framework,

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.250
but done a number of
different meetups,

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:26.040
and really expanded our presence
in that market and region.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.220
And on and on and on.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.230
Ubuntu just launched
with 1804 native support

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.430
for ML and Kubeflow.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.870
So we're really really excited
with all the contributors

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.560
so far.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.370
And then, finally, the
real end of the day

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.780
is, it's now an order
of minutes in order

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.040
to get an entire complicated,
distributed ML workload up

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:50.670
and running.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:51.570
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
On any Kubernetes.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.590
DAVID ARONCHICK: On any
Kubernetes-compliant platform.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:54.760
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah,
conformant Kubernetes.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:56.468
So if you've got a
conformant Kubernetes,

00:02:56.468 --> 00:02:58.650
you can deploy Kubeflow
on top of that.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:02:59.691
DAVID ARONCHICK: Exactly.

00:02:59.691 --> 00:03:01.860
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And as
simple as a Kubectl command?

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:02.901
DAVID ARONCHICK: Exactly.

00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:04.640
Even better than that, actually.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.030
Though we, obviously, only use
native Kubernetes components,

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.832
we actually detail
using ksonnet.

00:03:10.832 --> 00:03:12.040
There's another contributor--

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.456
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Oh, there you go.

00:03:13.456 --> 00:03:15.800
DAVID ARONCHICK: --from
Heptio, helping us use ksonnet

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.030
for our application deployment.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.474
You literally list in code
the declarative framework

00:03:21.474 --> 00:03:22.890
that you want to
get out the door.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.250
So let's say I want to use
components 1, 7, and 8.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.824
You declare that in code.

00:03:27.824 --> 00:03:30.240
You can check that code into
Git, just like anything else,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.700
and use that as your
deployment framework, --

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:33.990
and Kubernetes does the rest.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:38.080
So no more pip install
A, pip install B,

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:39.955
I forgot this
dependency isn't there--

00:03:39.955 --> 00:03:42.330
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: --and good
luck reproducing that, right?

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.980
DAVID ARONCHICK: And
good luck reproducing it.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.460
All that is built
into the system.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:46.960
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Yeah, that's great.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.800
So it's really
changing the investment

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:53.720
that a given data
scientist has to put down

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.220
to start doing the work that's
actually interesting to them,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.744
which is the machine
learning algorithms, right?

00:03:58.744 --> 00:03:59.910
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.490
And while there's plenty
of opportunity to get

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:06.330
started on a day 0
where you click a button

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.970
and it spins up a hosted
Jupyter Notebook or something

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:10.240
like that--

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.470
which is certainly fine if
you just want to mess around--

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.610
what we find is that,
more often than not,

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.480
these data scientists who want
to get that great first start

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.570
experience then end up
having to rewrite everything

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.870
to make it production-ready
and bring it from your laptop

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.570
or bring it from cloud
one to cloud two.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.400
Kubeflow runs great everyone.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:30.480
It runs on your laptop.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.150
It runs on your
training rig, on-prem.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.160
It runs on cloud one.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.320
It runs on cloud two.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.230
Anywhere there's a Kubernetes
conformant configuration--

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.012
cluster, excuse me--
it'll run great.

00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:41.220
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And do you
have any sample workloads

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:44.460
that can be deployed,
today, into Kubeflow as--

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:45.810
kind of getting started?

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.250
DAVID ARONCHICK:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:50.070
We have a great examples
directory where people

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:51.690
are already contributing.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.420
As those of you that
saw the keynote saw,

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.020
we demonstrated the
tensor-to-tensor framework

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.630
model that runs inside
Kubeflow right now.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.460
And that did raw
sentiment analysis, which

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:04.870
is an extremely common problem.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.210
People want to measure
inbound emails,

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:10.660
figure out how mad people
are, look at Twitter streams,

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:14.620
look at comments, and be
able to judge what's good

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.720
and what's bad and
what needs surfacing.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.332
So that model is all built
in, and it runs great.

00:05:19.332 --> 00:05:20.290
It runs on your laptop.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:21.210
It runs on a GPU.

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.080
It runs on TPUs, as
you saw on the stage.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.080
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Gotcha.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.970
Can I run Not Hotdog from
HBO's "Silicon Valley"?

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:28.064
Tim Anglade's--

00:05:28.064 --> 00:05:29.230
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.090
Actually, as part of
the tensor-to-tensor,

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:33.790
there is image
detection built in.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:36.849
But we also support
ResNet, ImageNet, and a lot

00:05:36.849 --> 00:05:38.140
of the other common frameworks.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:39.098
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Right.

00:05:39.098 --> 00:05:43.364
So you're really bringing AIML
to a cloud-native discipline.

00:05:43.364 --> 00:05:45.030
I'm actually really
impressed with this.

00:05:45.030 --> 00:05:48.580
I think this is a really
important step forward

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.500
into letting data scientists
worry about the problems

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.790
that they worry about, and
IT administrators and app

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.720
developers handle their own.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.630
DAVID ARONCHICK: Yeah,
I couldn't agree more.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:00.970
More than anything,
Kubernetes has been about,

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:02.740
how do we break
apart the things that

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.240
are tightly merged together?

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.460
Because that just helps
people move much faster.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.230
And then your IT pros can
focus on what they do great,

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.730
and your software engineers can
focus on what they do great,

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:14.230
and your data
scientists can focus on

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:15.220
and what they do great.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:18.610
And all of them together
can empower all the people

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:22.660
in the world to make great
decisions about where they need

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:27.130
to move resources around, what
people are being under-served

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.760
by services, and
so on and so forth.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:34.060
The more that we can break
those things apart and advance

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.550
each of those disciplines,
I think the better off

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.012
the platform is.

00:06:38.012 --> 00:06:39.970
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: From a
Kubernetes perspective,

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:42.700
do you think we can
lead here by helping

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:46.720
use ML from the
exhaust that comes out

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:49.630
of a Kubernetes cluster,
the logs and monitoring?

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:51.340
Can we actually
ingest that and start

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:53.950
developing some predictive
analytics for Kubernetes

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:54.460
itself?

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:57.010
Is this an opportunity
to reinforce

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:58.044
what we're doing here?

00:06:58.044 --> 00:06:59.460
DAVID ARONCHICK:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.940
So just like Kubernetes was an
externalization of the things

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:07.540
that we had done at
Google for 10, 15 years,

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:10.290
Kubeflow is very much
an externalization

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:12.220
of some of the pipelines
and other stacks

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:18.640
that we have internally that
we use at Google for ML.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.000
We're not even close
to the end here,

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.330
as those that saw the
TensorFlow DevSummit saw.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:27.970
There's a great platform
effort around what's

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:30.220
called TFX, TensorFlow
Extended, which will

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:34.150
appear in Kubeflow over time.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:36.220
But directly to the
answer to your question--

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.590
internally to Google,
we do that all the time.

00:07:38.590 --> 00:07:41.680
We look at our version
of Kubernetes, Borg,

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.520
and we use ML to study
jobs and see what's working

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.270
and what's not and all
that kind of stuff.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:49.270
Using that same kind of
anonymoly-- excuse me,

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.910
anomaly detection
as part of Kubeflow

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:53.484
is definitely a core mission.

00:07:53.484 --> 00:07:54.400
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:55.990
That's great.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:59.320
Since you are ultimately
a user of Kubernetes--

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.330
I mean, you're
running a project that

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:05.740
sits on top of Kubernetes--
surely you've got some hope,

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:08.470
some wishes, some plans
for Kubernetes itself.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:11.800
Can you share back with us
where some of the big problems

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:13.684
are left to solve in Kubernetes?

00:08:13.684 --> 00:08:14.600
DAVID ARONCHICK: Yeah.

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:18.414
You know, I've seen it grow so
much over the past three years.

00:08:18.414 --> 00:08:19.330
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Sure.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:21.300
Given your past as
well, you are the person

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.300
to ask this question to.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:23.995
DAVID ARONCHICK:
Well, I will say

00:08:23.995 --> 00:08:28.120
it's difficult to overstate the
amount of traction and distance

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.790
they've gotten in these
really core areas--

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:33.789
storage, networking,
multi-tenancy.

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:35.750
These are really hard problems.

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:38.500
And there's still miles
to go, but they've

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.667
made enormous progress.

00:08:40.667 --> 00:08:42.250
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Which of those do you

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:45.984
need at the heart of Kubeflow
to sort of evolve and mature?

00:08:45.984 --> 00:08:46.900
DAVID ARONCHICK: Well.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:49.180
I think more than anything,
what we'd really like

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:52.480
is continued investment
by each of the people

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.120
who are great at those solutions
and clean layers between them.

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.530
So you take folks
like NVIDIA, who

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.325
are contributing
around accelerators,

00:09:01.325 --> 00:09:03.514
and you take people
like Rook, who

00:09:03.514 --> 00:09:05.680
are contributing around
storage, and you take people

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:07.900
like Cisco, who are
contributing around networking.

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:10.350
Those people are going to
be experts at what they do.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.450
And as long as they plug
into the clean interfaces

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:16.180
that Kubernetes
provides, then Kubeflow

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:19.290
can consume all of that
goodness without having

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.040
to know anything about
it-- without having

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:23.440
to know which version
of Driver is doing this,

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:25.120
or what private
network is doing that.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.660
We're just going to use
what's already provided to us.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:31.190
So more than anything, I'd just
love everyone who's experts--

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:33.760
whether or not it's a different
cloud or different hardware

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:36.280
system or a different
software stack that

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:37.540
underlies this stuff--

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:40.780
to continue to invest, make
it run great with Kubernetes,

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.980
and invest in the interfaces
that go on top of it.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:44.979
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: What
brought you to Kubernetes?

00:09:44.979 --> 00:09:47.530
Can you tell us
just how you came

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:50.230
to be a product manager
on Kubernetes itself?

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:51.550
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:56.000
So I was, at the time, working
at Chef in enterprise software.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.470
I was a senior director of
product marketing there.

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:03.150
And I happen to know
Craig McLuckie, co-founder

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:04.350
of the Kubernetes project.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:08.490
And he is a persistent dude.

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:11.799
So we just started
talking, and he kept

00:10:11.799 --> 00:10:13.090
saying, "You've got to come in.

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:13.920
You've got to do this thing."

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:16.040
And I was like, wow, this
Kubernetes thing really

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.090
is starting to be something.

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.964
And I joined up shortly after.

00:10:19.964 --> 00:10:20.880
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Cool.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:21.880
So Craig recruited you.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:22.950
DAVID ARONCHICK: Oh, absolutely.

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:23.670
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Awesome.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:25.590
Well, speaking of Craig
and the community,

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:28.465
is there anyone else
in the community--

00:10:28.465 --> 00:10:30.840
there's a ton of great people
in the community, but maybe

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.195
your opportunity to
send a thanks out

00:10:33.195 --> 00:10:35.730
or a shout out to
someone who inspires you

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.070
in the Kubernetes community.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.860
DAVID ARONCHICK: Boy,
there really are a ton.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:42.510
When I think about
it, there's so

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:45.940
many people who deserve an
enormous amount of credit.

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.566
I think there are people who
are really doing a lot of great

00:10:48.566 --> 00:10:50.940
work bridging the gap between
those who are familiar with

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:52.981
Kubernetes and containers,
and those who aren't--

00:10:52.981 --> 00:10:56.040
people like Kelsey Hightower,
Jessie Frazelle, and so on.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:00.030
I think there are those
who are keeping the trains

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:03.990
running, the unsung heroes--

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:08.010
Paris, Sarah Novotny, all
the community managers.

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:09.900
It's incredibly
difficult to overstate

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.090
how valuable that glue is.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:14.750
And then there are the real--

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:17.250
oh geez, the people
who've been on it forever,

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:21.420
who still show up and pay
attention to GitHub issues

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:23.970
and do all the hard work--

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:26.160
Brian Grant, Tim
Hockin, Eric Tune.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.660
On and on and on.

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:30.060
They're just absolutely
out there every day,

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:32.820
still answering these
questions and keeping

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:35.490
things architecturally pure.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:38.460
I wish I could deserve
one iota of credit here.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:42.000
I think if there was one
thing that everyone said,

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.700
it was how well thought
through and well-designed

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.710
the core APIs of Kubernetes are.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:49.817
And that's really
been the reason

00:11:49.817 --> 00:11:51.900
that all these things--
that we can build Kubeflow

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:55.630
without having to fork, because
the APIs and the core platform

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:56.130
is so solid.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:56.880
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
It's amazing how far

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:59.010
we've come in such a
short amount of time.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.120
DAVID ARONCHICK: Oh, yeah.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:01.240
Very, very difficult to believe.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:02.823
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
one last question.

00:12:02.823 --> 00:12:04.800
As you think about
young engineers who

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:08.370
are university
students who are seeing

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:10.260
our community from the
outside and wanting

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:11.920
to be a part of
this, do you have

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:15.300
any advice for
those people and how

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.030
to get involved in Kubernetes?

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:19.680
DAVID ARONCHICK: Yeah.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:23.280
I know it will feel,
to a lot of folks,

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:27.785
like it is an overwhelming
amount of information

00:12:27.785 --> 00:12:29.910
that you need to pick
up and help with,

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:32.790
and geez, where
do I get started?

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:35.970
I'll say what I've been saying
for the past three years.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:38.640
There's a "help wanted" label.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:40.950
Literally any one of those
"help wanted" labels,

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:42.180
you can go and pick up.

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:45.390
You can learn as well as
anyone and get started.

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:48.120
And whether or not that's
documentation, or fixing

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:52.110
an error code, or actually
fixing a unit test,

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:55.140
all the way up to
redesigning an interface,

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:59.660
the community is ready to accept
your help and get started.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:05.190
And so I guess my one advice
would be diving into GitHub,

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:08.190
find an area where someone
is already raising their hand

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:10.420
and saying "I would
love some help."

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:12.690
And then, don't hesitate
to ask questions.

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:15.870
There will be someone out there
who's ready to respond and get

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:16.530
you going.

00:13:16.530 --> 00:13:17.430
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: That's right.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:19.090
Well, David, thank
you for your time.

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:20.690
DAVID ARONCHICK: My pleasure.

